# Copy this file to `config.env` at the checkout root (next to reconnect.sh --
# not in this directory) and fill in your real values, from the root:
#     cp manual-setup/config.env.example config.env
#     chmod 600 config.env      # keep the password readable only by you
#
# `config.env` is listed in .gitignore so your password is never committed.
#
# This file is PARSED, not run as a shell script. Values are taken literally:
# `$`, backticks and backslashes mean nothing special, so a password like
# P@$$w0rd`x` works as written. Spaces around the `=` are ignored, so
# `WIFI_SSID = "MyNet"` and `WIFI_SSID="MyNet"` are the same. Wrap a value in
# quotes to keep leading or trailing spaces inside it; a `#` inside a value is
# part of the value, not a comment. An unrecognised setting is an error, so a
# typo shows up straight away, and so is the same setting given twice: this file
# is read top to bottom, so a forgotten second line would otherwise win without
# saying so.

# The name (SSID) of the Wi-Fi network to stay connected to.
# Must contain at least one non-blank character.
WIFI_SSID=""

# The password for that network.
#
# Optional if NetworkManager already has a saved profile for this SSID -- for
# example because you connected once through the desktop UI. In that case the
# script uses the saved profile and never needs the password. See SECURITY.md.
WIFI_PASSWORD=""

# Optional: the Wi-Fi interface to use (e.g. wlan0, wlp3s0).
# Leave empty to let NetworkManager pick the default Wi-Fi device.
# Find yours with:  nmcli device status
WIFI_IFACE=""

# Optional: set to yes if the network does not broadcast its SSID.
# A hidden network never appears in a scan, so the script skips the scan wait
# and asks NetworkManager to connect to it directly.
WIFI_HIDDEN="no"
